I did this quick pair of runs to establish the benefit of parallelism (by tx within a block) for `tx.set_strong()` , which precedes confirmation evaluation. The store is fully concurrent/thread safe read/write. So I just dropped in this one change (adding `bc::par_unseq`), and got the graphed result for blocks 50k-190k:
```TEMPLATE
bool CLASS::set_strong(const header_link&amp; link) NOEXCEPT
{
    const auto txs = to_txs(link);
    if (txs.empty())
        return false;

    const table::strong_tx::record strong{ {}, link };

    // ========================================================================
    const auto scope = store_.get_transactor();

    return std::all_of(bc::par_unseq, txs.begin(), txs.end(),
        [&amp;](const tx_link&amp; fk) NOEXCEPT
        {
            return store_.strong_tx.put(fk, strong);
        });
    // ========================================================================
}```

